# recordings.conf.example — copy this to recordings.conf and edit it
# with your real values. The real file (recordings.conf, no suffix) is
# gitignored and never committed, since it names a real remote host and
# path. This .example file has no real data in it, so it's safe to keep
# in git as a reference template.
#
#     cp recordings.conf.example recordings.conf
#     nano recordings.conf
#
# Format (pipe-delimited, one scheduled pull per line):
#
#     mountpoint | OnCalendar_expr | pull_minutes | user@host | remote_path
#
# mountpoint     Must match an existing entry in channels.conf, AND be
#                listed here for 06-streaming.sh to actually feed the
#                RAM ring buffer for it — being in channels.conf alone
#                is not enough; a channel is only buffered if it's also
#                listed in this file.
#
# OnCalendar_expr  Standard systemd calendar syntax — when this schedule
#                  fires and pulls from the buffer. Test any expression
#                  with: systemd-analyze calendar '<expr>'
#                    *-*-* 14:00:00      -> every day at 14:00
#                    Mon *-*-* 08:00:00  -> every Monday at 08:00
#                    Sun *-*-* 20:30:00  -> every Sunday at 20:30
#
# pull_minutes   How far back into the RAM ring buffer to reach when
#                this fires — NOT a live recording duration. Must not
#                exceed the buffer's actual retention window
#                (BUFFER_RETENTION_MIN in lib/common.sh, 60 minutes by
#                default) — a request for more fails loudly at setup
#                time rather than silently returning less than asked.
#
# user@host      SSH destination for the push. Must contain '@'.
#                The public key shown during ./09-recording.sh setup
#                must already be in this account's ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
#                on the remote system.
#
# remote_path    Destination directory on that host.
#
# Comments: a '#' starts a comment anywhere on a line — whole-line or
# trailing after real data — everything from it to end of line is
# ignored. Blank lines are ignored too.

# --- Example — replace with your real channel/schedule/destination ---
# ch1 | Sun *-*-* 20:00:00 | 60 | archiveuser@backup.example.org | /data/hf-recordings
